Four different OMW samples from the region of Western Greece were used as feedstock for the anaerobic digestion systems (Fig. 1
                        ). The first OMW sample was collected from the region of Kerkyra, Greece (Sample 1), the second from the region of Agrinio, Greece (Sample 3), the third from the region of Kalamata, Greece (Sample 4), and the fourth from the region of Patras, Achaia, Greece (Sample 5). Moreover, liquid waste from the horizontal decanter of a pomace oil production facility using two-phase pomace as feedstock in the region of Pyrgos, Greece (Sample 2) was also examined. All the samples were collected during the peak of olive oil production period (November/December 2019). A wide variation in the characteristics of OMW has been observed in the literature, depending on the type of the olive tree, the way of its cultivation, the season of the olive harvest, and the practices followed during the harvest and production of the olive oil at the olive mill. The characterization of OMW is of utmost importance, since the suitability of the substrate for the proposed process can be determined. The main characteristics of the waste samples that were used as substrates are presented in Table 1
                        .
